    "field": "Geoengineering",
    "problem": "Solar radiation management schemes that use a simple, uniform reduction in solar radiation inadequately compensate for CO2-induced warming, creating residual climate changes and regional inequalities because the spatial and temporal patterns of the two forcings do not match.",
    "method": "The paper proposes a constrained least-squares optimization method, which uses a linear superposition of general circulation model climate responses, to determine the optimal spatio-temporal distribution of solar radiation reduction required to manage trade-offs between different climate objectives.",
    "task": "The task is to demonstrate how optimizing the spatial and seasonal distribution of solar radiation reduction can more effectively offset anthropogenic climate change while managing the trade-offs between different objectives, such as reducing regional inequalities and minimizing side-effects.",
